# Can a tax fix pollution?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How does a Pigouvian tax make the polluter feel the cost? How would the government know the right size?"
next: {"title":"Or could property rights fix it?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/44-or-could-property-rights-fix-it.cast"}

## Paint in the harbour
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Hulls painted a year (Q)
    y_label: Price per hull (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
      offset: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
      offset: 1
      label: Yard's cost
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      q_label: '100'
      p_label: $1,000
      shift_q_label: '72'
      shift_p_label: ''
      shift_label: E′
    supply_shift:
      amount: -28
      label: Social cost
      arrow: vertical
    units:
      price: [0, 2000]
      quantity: [0, 200]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: hulls
sources:
    - id: pigou1920
      title: The Economics of Welfare
      authors: A. C. Pigou
      year: 1920
      finding: Costs thrown on people not directly concerned, such as uncompensated damage to surrounding woods by sparks from railway engines, make private and social net product diverge; a tax or bounty can correct it.
    - id: murray_rivers
      title: 'British Columbia''s revenue-neutral carbon tax: A review of the latest ''grand experiment'' in environmental policy'
      authors: Brian C. Murray, Nicholas Rivers
      year: 2015
      finding: The tax started at C$10 per tonne CO2e in 2008 and rose to C$30 by 2012, with revenue returned through other tax cuts; empirical and simulation studies suggest it reduced emissions in the province by 5–15% relative to what they would have been.
      doi: 10.1016/j.enpol.2015.08.011
    - id: bc2025
      title: B.C. eliminates consumer carbon tax (news release 2025FIN0014-000280)
      authors: Government of British Columbia, Ministry of Finance
      year: 2025
      finding: The consumer carbon tax rate was set to zero from April 1, 2025, the same day the federal consumer carbon-pricing requirement ended.
      url: https://news.gov.bc.ca/releases/2025FIN0014-000280

    scratch bc cites ["murray_rivers","bc2025"] work ["British Columbia's carbon tax","2008: C$10 a tonne of CO₂","2012: C$30, money handed back","emissions: −5 to 15% (estimate)","2025: scrapped for consumers"] x 500 y 420 font_size 42 hidden true
    text harm "+$500 harm" #b5482e at.data [20,36] font_size 30 hidden true
    region over x_from 36 x_to 50 between ["supply_shift_curve","demand_curve"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text too_many "28 too many" #b5482e at.data [40,88] font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait pigou cites ["pigou1920"] x 810 y 455 width 150 of "Arthur Cecil Pigou" cameo true hidden true
    text pigou_tax "Pigouvian tax:\npay for the harm" #8a5fa8 cites ["pigou1920"] x 860 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Paint in the harbour"

Can a tax fix pollution? In 2008 British Columbia bet that it could, with a tax on every tonne of carbon dioxide its fuels gave off.
    draw bc_box bc_line_1 bc_line_2

B: Ten Canadian dollars  (@draw bc_line_3@)a tonne at first, thirty by 2012, and the money handed back as cuts to other taxes.

Did it work? The best estimates say emissions f (@draw bc_line_4@)ell five to fifteen percent below where they would otherwise have been.

B: And yet in 2025 the pro (@draw bc_line_5@)vince scrapped it for consumers. So why should a tax help at all, and how big should it be?

    erase bc

Take Har (@draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@)bourtown's boatyard, with new illustrative numbers: the paint it puts on hulls wa (@draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift supply_shift_arrow harm@)shes into the harbour, and costs the fishers five hundred dollars a hull.

B: So the market paints a hundred hull (@draw shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift over too_many@)s where the town, counting the fish, would pick seventy-two: twenty-eight too many.

About a century ago, the Cambri (@draw pigou@)dge economist Arthur Pigou worried about the same thing: sparks from steam trains setting fire to the woods along the line.

    erase pigou

His answer: charge the polluter, per unit, for the  (@draw pigou_tax@)harm it does to others. We call it a Pigouvian tax.

    quiz "How big a tax per hull gets Harbourtown to the right number of hulls?" judge false voice b
        intro: And most people's instinct is that pollution is bad, so you tax it as hard as you can.
        right: Hold on to that guess: the figure tests it next.
        * $100
        * $500
        * $1,000
        * So high nobody paints

Whatever the size, the tax has one job: close the gap between what the yard pays and what the town pays.
    highlight supply_shift_arrow harm effect pulse

    pause 0.5

## A tax that removes a loss
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Hulls painted a year (Q)
    y_label: Price per hull (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
      offset: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
      offset: 1
      label: Yard's cost
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      q_label: '100'
      p_label: $1,000
      shift_q_label: '72'
      shift_p_label: ''
      shift_label: E′
    supply_shift:
      amount: -28
      label: Social cost
      arrow: vertical
    tax:
      amount: 0
      side: seller
      label: + tax
    units:
      price: [0, 2000]
      quantity: [0, 200]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: hulls
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 110
      w: 740
      h: 580

    text ro_q "Traded: {market.quantity_traded} hulls" x 872 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ro_b "Buyers pay: ${market.price_buyers}" #b5482e x 872 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ro_s "Yard gets: ${market.price_sellers}" #2f6b8f x 872 y 320 font_size 34 hidden true
    region over x_from 36 x_to 50 between ["supply_shift_curve","demand_curve"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    region under x_from 22 x_to 36 between ["supply_shift_curve","demand_curve"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work ["Yard stops where:",{"tex":"P = MC + t"},"Town would stop where:",{"tex":"P = MC + MD"},"Same Q only if:",{"tex":"t = MD \\text{ at the best } Q"}] x 500 y 400 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "A tax that removes a loss"

Now the guess meets the figure. The gap between the yard's cost and the t (@draw supply_shift_arrow@)own's cost is five hundred dollars a hull, so try a tax of exactly that.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar supply_shift_curve label_S_shift shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift over parallel true

Every hull now carrie (@animate tax.amount 25 duration 3@)s five hundred dollars of tax, and the yard's cost curve rises, right onto the town's.
    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax ro_q ro_b ro_s

B: So the yard pays for the fishers' loss as if it were its own bill.
    highlight tax_supply_curve

And it now paints seventy-t (@draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines@)wo hulls: (@highlight ro_q@) the town's own choice. Nobody had to tell it how many.

B: Boat owners pa (@draw price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps@)y twelve hundred and fifty dollars, the yard keeps seven (@highlight ro_b ro_s@) hundred and fifty, and the council takes the five hundred in between.

B: But hold on. Taxes usually waste something: trades that no longer happen.
    highlight over effect pulse

This one removes a waste instead. The trades it st (@erase over@)ops are the twenty-eight hulls that cost the town more than they were worth.

Try it: drag the taxed curve up and down, and watch where the yard's choice lands against the town's best point.
    explore

    quiz "The council overshoots: $1,000 a hull, twice the harm. Compared with no tax at all, the town is…" voice b
        right: About as badly off: with straight-line curves like these, the yard paints twenty-eight hulls too few, a loss as big as twenty-eight too many.
        wrong: Count how many hulls now fall short of seventy-two, and compare with the twenty-eight too many: here, about as badly off.
        * Better off
        + About as badly off
        * Worse off

Forty-four hulls: twenty-eight too few. Hulls worth more than th (@draw under@)eir full cost to the town now go unpainted.
    animate tax.amount 50 duration 2.5 ghost true

So the right tax is neither the smallest nor t (@erase under@)he biggest. It is the harm done by the last hull, and that one number is what the council has to get right.
    animate tax.amount 25 duration 2

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own intro "Want the rule behind it?" voice b

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar supply_shift_curve label_S_shift supply_shift_arrow shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps ro_q ro_b ro_s to 0.12

The yard paints until the price just covers its own cost plus the tax.
    draw m_box m_line_1 m_line_2

The town would stop where the price just covers the cost plus MD, the marginal damage: the harm from one more hull.
    draw m_line_3 m_line_4

They stop at the same hull only when t equals MD, measured at that best hull, not at today's.
    draw m_line_5 m_line_6

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar supply_shift_curve label_S_shift supply_shift_arrow shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps ro_q ro_b ro_s to 1

    pause 0.5

## How would they know?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '2021: 3%'
      - '2023: 2.5%'
      - '2023: 2%'
      - '2023: 1.5%'
    values:
      - 51
      - 120
      - 190
      - 340
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    colors:
      - grey
      - blue
      - blue
      - blue
    x_label: 'US estimate: year and discount rate'
    y_label: $ per tonne of CO₂
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 150
      w: 560
      h: 390
    label_size: 22
sources:
    - id: iwg2021
      title: 'Technical Support Document: Social Cost of Carbon, Methane, and Nitrous Oxide — Interim Estimates under Executive Order 13990'
      authors: Interagency Working Group on Social Cost of Greenhouse Gases, United States Government
      year: 2021
      finding: 'Social cost of CO2 for 2020 emissions: $51 per metric ton (2020 dollars, 3% discount rate).'
    - id: epa2023
      title: 'Report on the Social Cost of Greenhouse Gases: Estimates Incorporating Recent Scientific Advances'
      authors: US Environmental Protection Agency
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Social cost of CO2 for 2020 emissions, 2020 dollars per metric ton: $120 (2.5% near-term discount rate), $190 (2%, central), $340 (1.5%).'
      url: https://www.epa.gov/environmental-economics/scghg
    - id: plumer2018
      title: Trump Put a Low Cost on Carbon Emissions. Here's Why It Matters.
      authors: Brad Plumer (New York Times)
      year: 2018
      finding: The Trump administration's estimate counted only domestic damages and used 3% and 7% discount rates, giving roughly $1 to $7 per ton.
    - id: baumol1971
      title: The Use of Standards and Prices for Protection of the Environment
      authors: W. J. Baumol and W. E. Oates
      year: 1971
      finding: 'Since marginal damages are hard to measure, choose an acceptable standard and set charges, adjusted by trial, to meet it: the standard is reached at least cost.'
    - id: murray2015
      title: 'British Columbia''s revenue-neutral carbon tax: A review of the latest ''grand experiment'' in environmental policy'
      authors: B. Murray and N. Rivers
      year: 2015
      finding: Empirical and simulation studies suggest the tax cut emissions in the province by 5–15%, with negligible effects on aggregate economic performance.
    - id: omb2025
      title: 'M-25-27: Guidance Implementing Section 6 of Executive Order 14154, Entitled "Unleashing American Energy"'
      authors: US Office of Management and Budget
      year: 2025
      finding: EO 14154 (January 2025) disbanded the Interagency Working Group and withdrew its social-cost-of-greenhouse-gas estimates; the guidance says the calculation is no longer uniform and agencies should not use it in regulatory or permitting decisions.
      url: https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/M-25-27-Guidance-Implementing-Section-6-of-Executive-Order-14154-Entitled-Unleashing-American-Energy.pdf

    text rule "Tax = harm of the last unit" x 400 y 630 font_size 40 hidden true
    text unknown "harm = ?" #b5482e x 830 y 600 font_size 64 hidden true
    text earlier "Before 2021,\nUS harm only:\n$1–7" #8f887c cites ["plumer2018"] x 830 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text bo "Baumol & Oates:\npick a target,\nadjust the tax" #8a5fa8 cites ["baumol1971"] x 830 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sure1 "Price cuts pollution:\nmostly agreed\n(BC carbon tax: −5 to 15%)" #2f6b8f cites ["murray2015"] x 830 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sure2 "Right size:\ncontested" #b5482e x 830 y 200 font_size 36 hidden true
    text dropped "2025: US drops\nthe figure" #b5482e cites ["omb2025"] x 830 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text know "fish lost?\ntheir price?\nhow harm grows with paint?" #8f887c x 420 y 400 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "How would they know?"

B: That five hundred dollars was easy to get right. You made it up.
    draw rule

Fair. A real council needs the harm (@draw unknown@) of the last hull at the best quantity, and it doesn't know that quantity either.

B: So it has to know  (@draw know@)the fish lost, their price, how the harm grows with more paint. Things nobody at the council can see.

    erase know

Climate is the big real case. A tonne of carbon dioxide does harm all over the world, for centuries. What is that worth?
    draw axes

In 2021, for instance, the US gover (@draw bar_1@)nment's working figure was fifty-one dollars a tonne.

B: In 2023 its environm (@draw bar_3@)ent agency said a hundred and ninety. Or a hundr (@draw bar_2@)ed and twenty, or three  (@draw bar_4@)hundred and forty, depending on one choice.

The choice is how much weight to give people living a century from now. A few years earlier, counting (@draw earlier@) only harm inside the US, the figure had been one to seven dollars.
    highlight axes

    erase earlier

B: And in 2025 the US (@draw dropped@) government stopped using a figure at all.

    erase dropped

B: In 1971 William Baumol and Wallace Oates suggeste (@draw bo@)d a way round: skip the damage estimate. Pick a target, set a tax, and raise or lower it until the target is met.

That reaches the target at the lowest cost. Whether it's the right target, the tax can't tell you.
    highlight unknown

    erase bo

How sure are we? That a price on pollu (@draw sure1@)tion cuts it is mostly agreed: British Columbia's estimated five to fifteen percent is one of many such findings. What the ri (@draw sure2@)ght price is stays contested.

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 sure1 sure2

    quiz "Each extra car trip into a city centre does $3 of harm to others, at the efficient number of trips. What charge per trip?" intro "Three new cases." reveal "$3 a trip"
        right: $3: the harm of the last trip. More would price out trips worth making.
        wrong: The charge should match the harm one more trip does to others: here, $3.
        * $0
        + $3
        * As high as possible

    quiz "A government guesses carbon's harm at $50 a tonne, but the true harm is $150. With a $50 tax, emissions are…" reveal "Still too high" voice b
        right: Still too high: polluters feel only a third of the harm, so they cut less than they should, though more than with no tax.
        wrong: Does the polluter now feel all of the harm, or only a third of it? Here, still too high.
        + Still too high
        * Too low
        * Exactly right

    quiz "For which pollutant is one national tax rate closer to right?" reveal "CO₂: one rate fits"
        right: Carbon dioxide: a tonne does the same harm anywhere, so one rate can fit. Soot's harm changes street by street.
        wrong: Where the harm depends on the place, the right answer needs many rates: here, carbon dioxide.
        + Carbon dioxide, which does the same harm wherever it is released
        * Soot, which harms only the people nearby

So can a tax fix pollution? In principle, exactly. In practice, only as well as the government knows the harm, and how much a government can know is the question that comes next.
    highlight rule unknown

    pause 0.5

